{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"2843d4b0-3c99-49a6-8540-4c63c62de40a","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Ahead, wavefronts bunch together. Behind, they spread apart. The sound speed stays the same; the wavelength changes.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"motion","canvas_id":"waves","from":[0.5,0.12],"to":[0.72,0.12],"stroke":"solid","label":"motion"},{"op":"line","id":"rear_gap","canvas_id":"waves","from":[0.08,0.5],"to":[0.24,0.5],"stroke":"dashed","label":"longer λ"},{"op":"point","id":"listener","canvas_id":"waves","at":[0.86,0.5],"label":"listener"},{"op":"equation","id":"spacing","anchor":{"el":"assume","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\lambda_{\\mathrm{ahead}}=\\frac{c-v_s}{f_s},\\qquad \\lambda_{\\mathrm{behind}}=\\frac{c+v_s}{f_s}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"For the stationary listener ahead, will pulses arrive more or less frequently?","expected_gist":"More frequently: the shorter wavelength means a higher observed frequency."}}}
